  "answer": "Higher-authority sources—official performance metrics ([NL-001#NL-001-S04]) and the executive meeting record ([NL-015#NL-015-S03])—directly contradict the sales material's characterization of education startups as the best ICP. They indicate that the official ICP is concentrated in regulated mid-market and enterprise organizations, not SMBs or startups.\n\nSpecifically, the higher-authority sources show:\n\n- Official metrics demonstrate the ICP is regulated mid-market and enterprise companies, not SMBs/startups [NL-001#NL-001-S04].\n- SMBs (which include education startups) show significantly higher churn and lower expansion potential [NL-001#NL-001-S04].\n- In Q2, SMB monthly logo churn was 4.8% vs 2.1% for Mid-Market and 1.3% for Enterprise; expansion revenue was $18k for SMB vs $64k (Mid-Market) and $91k (Enterprise) [NL-015#NL-015-S03].\n- SMB NPS was 31 vs 47 (Mid-Market) and 52 (Enterprise) [NL-015#NL-015-S03].\n- The CEO explicitly directed that the ICP remains regulated mid-market and enterprise (500–10,000 employees) and rejected pivoting to SMB/startup sales that churn out in six months [NL-015#NL-015-S03].\n\nEven the sales material itself contains a caveat conceding that official Finance metrics (Q2 Business Metrics Report - NL-012) highlight low initial contract values and elevated churn risks for this category [NL-009#NL-009-S04].\n\nANSWER_STATUS: answer\nUSED_MEMORY_CLAIMS: none",
